{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let E be any finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space, equipped with its norm and Borel measurable structure; dimension zero is allowed. Let S be any measurable space. Let V : E -> R be twice continuously Frechet differentiable on all of E, and let kappa be a real number with kappa >= 1. Suppose, for every x,v in E, that\n  kappa^(-1) ||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nLet eta : S -> R and y : S -> E be measurable, and suppose eta(s) > 0 for every s in S.\nThen there exists a measurable p : S -> E such that, for every s,\n  p(s) + eta(s) grad V(p(s)) = y(s),\nand, for every s and every z in E,\n  z + eta(s) grad V(z) = y(s) implies z = p(s).\nUsing this same p, define\n  rho_s(u) = V(p(s) + sqrt(eta(s)) u) - V(p(s))\n             - sqrt(eta(s)) <grad V(p(s)), u>,\n  mu = Tilt(volume_E, x |-> -V(x)),\n  R_s = Tilt(mu, x |-> -||x-y(s)||^2/(2 eta(s))),\n  T_s(x) = (sqrt(eta(s)))^(-1) (x-p(s)),\n  r_s = (T_s)_# R_s,\n  gamma = stdGaussian(E).\nHere Tilt is the normalized exponential tilt of the indicated measure by the indicated real potential, and gamma is the canonical centered variance-one Gaussian measure on E. For every s in S, r_s is a probability measure, r_s is absolutely continuous with respect to gamma, its canonical ENNReal KL divergence KL(r_s || gamma) is not infinity, and the actual globally defined gradient grad rho_s belongs to L^2(r_s), including almost-everywhere strong measurability and finiteness of its L^2 seminorm. Moreover,\n  toReal(KL(r_s || gamma)) <= (1/2) integral_E ||grad rho_s(u)||^2 dr_s(u),\nwhere the integral is the real Bochner integral of the squared Hilbert norm. The statement imposes no upper bound on eta, and supplies rather than assumes the measurable unique stationary witness p.",
  "reconstructed_text_sha256": "d10261f5045d29b304304f026bdc14d3b9c097461d709b06033fd3b18f9adcc4",
  "objects": "V is a real potential; kappa is a real curvature parameter. eta is a positive real measurable parameter function and y is an E-valued measurable parameter function. p is the produced measurable E-valued function solving the stationary equation uniquely pointwise. rho_s is the actual residual potential defined around that same p(s). mu is canonical volume exponentially tilted by -V and normalized. R_s is the actual subsequent normalized quadratic tilt of mu; r_s is its actual affine pushforward. gamma is stdGaussian E. KL is canonical ENNReal relative entropy. All gradients are the global Riesz inverse of the real Frechet derivative; D^2 V(x)[v,v] is the actual evaluated second derivative.",
  "domains": "E: arbitrary finite-dimensional complete real inner-product normed additive group with Borel measurable structure, including rank zero. S: arbitrary measurable parameter space with no measure required. V:E->R; eta:S->R; y,p:S->E; rho:S->E->R; mu,R_s,r_s,gamma: measures on E; x,v,z,u:E; kappa:R.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally over E,S and their displayed structures, V,kappa, and eta,y satisfying the assumptions; there exists one measurable p:S->E. Its stationary equation holds for every s. Uniqueness quantifies over every s and every z:E satisfying that equation. The measures and residual potential are defined using the same existential p. Each probability, absolute-continuity, KL-finiteness, L2-membership, and entropy inequality conclusion holds for every s, not merely almost every parameter.",
  "assumptions": "kappa>=1; V is globally C^2 over R; for all x,v:E, kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2; eta and y are measurable; eta(s)>0 for every s. Finite dimensionality, completeness, real inner-product geometry, and the Borel measurable structure on E are explicit ambient hypotheses. No upper step-size bound or uniform bounds on eta or y are supplied.",
  "conclusion": "Existence of a measurable p solving p(s)+eta(s)grad V(p(s))=y(s), with uniqueness against every E-valued solution at each s. After defining rho,mu,R,r,gamma as displayed, for each s: IsProbabilityMeasure(r_s); r_s << gamma; canonical ENNReal KL(r_s||gamma) != infinity; grad rho_s is MemLp 2 with respect to r_s; and toReal(KL(r_s||gamma)) <= (1/2) integral ||grad rho_s||^2 dr_s.",
  "scopes": "All Hessian bounds and differentiability are global on E. The parameter conclusions are pointwise for every s. Uniqueness concerns the stationary equation. Measure.tilted denotes normalized exponential tilting, not an unnormalized measure. r is the pushforward under the literal inverse-square-root scale and centering by p; gamma is the literal standard Gaussian. MemLp entails AE strong measurability and finite L2 seminorm of the actual gradient, not a selected gradient of a logarithmic Radon-Nikodym representative. The Fisher-side quantity is the real Bochner integral. Finite ENNReal KL is established separately before its toReal value is used. No source comparison or proof validation is asserted.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The Hessian upper constant is exactly 1 and its lower constant is exactly kappa inverse, with kappa>=1. The quadratic tilt denominator is exactly 2 eta(s). Residual scaling is exactly sqrt(eta(s)); pushforward scaling is its reciprocal. Gaussian centering is zero and variance normalization is one. The entropy multiplier is exactly 1/2, independent of s, dimension, kappa, eta, y, and V. The function p and resulting rho,R,r depend on V,eta,y; mu depends on V and canonical volume; gamma depends only on E.",
